Setting up your physical SAN storage controllers properly is the foundation for high-performance PostgreSQL infrastructure. Because database engines like Postgres heavily rely on predictable latency for write-ahead logging (pg_wal) and synchronous writes, poor SAN controller configuration will choke performance, no matter how much you tune the parameters inside the database or virtual layers.To maximize I/O throughput and eliminate queuing latency, configure your SAN controllers according to these architectural best practices.1. Optimize Controller Cache Policies (The Highest Impact)The way your storage controllers utilize their internal volatile memory (cache) drastically changes transactional latency.Enable Write-Back Cache (with Battery/SuperCap Protection): Ensure your storage arrays are configured for Write-Back cache instead of Write-Through. In Write-Back mode, the SAN controller acknowledges a write to PostgreSQL the millisecond the data hits the controller's memory pool, rather than waiting for it to be physically committed to flash or disk platter. This turns heavy, serial fsync operations from millisecond bottlenecks into microsecond bursts.⚠️ Mandatory Guardrail: Only enable Write-Back cache if your controllers feature redundant, fully tested Battery Backed Units (BBUs) or flash-backed SuperCap systems. If power drops without memory protection, data corruption on your database cluster is virtually guaranteed.Disable or Limit Read-Ahead (Pre-fetch) Cache:PostgreSQL typically reads data in random 8kB pages (unless executing a massive sequential table scan). Most SAN controller algorithms try to be smart by pre-fetching contiguous blocks of data into memory when they see a read request. For a transactional database workload, this creates massive cache pollution—wasting SAN cache capacity and host-fabric bandwidth loading blocks that Postgres will never ask for. Let Postgres handle its own pre-fetching via its effective_io_concurrency configuration.2. Isolate Fabric Paths and Enforce Active-Active ALUAYour SAN controllers should dynamically distribute traffic across all physical host ports without causing internal path thrashing.Enforce Asymmetric Logical Unit Access (ALUA): Configure your storage groups to present LUNs via ALUA. This ensures the VM host always knows which SAN controller natively owns the volume ("Active-Optimized") and passes I/O directly down those physical lines.Utilize All Available Ports: Ensure your physical host paths alternate systematically between Controller A and Controller B. Combined with the virtualization-level multipathing configuration (Round Robin with IOPS=1), this ensures you are pulling full aggregated bandwidth across all physical processor boards on the storage array.3. Build Dedicated Physical Pools (RAID Topologies)Even if you use virtualization, never dump your Postgres Write-Ahead Logs (pg_wal) and core Data Tablespaces onto the same underlying physical spindle/flash pool inside the SAN controller.Database ComponentI/O PatternOptimal SAN Pool ConfigurationWrite-Ahead Logs (pg_wal)Append-only, 100% Sequential Writes. High execution speed/low latency is vital.Isolate to a dedicated, small physical disk pool. RAID 10 or dedicated ultra-low-latency NVMe tiers are highly recommended. Avoid RAID 5/6 due to parity calculating overhead on continuous writes.Data Tablespaces (base)Random Reads & Random Writes mixed with intensive sequential bursts during checkpoints/scans.High capacity, balanced performance pool. RAID 10 is the gold standard for performance. If utilizing SSD/NVMe pools, RAID 5 or RAID 6 can be accepted only if the SAN controller handles parity tracking completely in dedicated hardware ASIC chips with sufficient write-back buffering.4. Match SAN Allocation Block SizesTo prevent a performance-killing phenomenon called Write Amplification, the physical layout of your SAN block sizes must match your database structure.PostgreSQL writes data in native 8kB pages.If your SAN controller's underlying storage pool allocates blocks at 64kB or 128kB stripes without clean page alignment, writing a single updated 8kB Postgres page forces the SAN controller to read a full 64kB block, alter the 8kB fragment in cache, and write the entire 64kB structure back down to disk.The Fix: Work with your storage administrator to configure thin-provisioned allocation granularities or volume block sizes that align naturally with database increments (such as 8kB or 16kB target sizes for high-performance volumes), reducing back-end parity and block manipulation overhead.5. Striping and LUN Fan-OutWhen creating virtual disks for your 20-mount-point configuration, do not allocate them out of a single massive LUN unless your SAN utilizes a modern wide-striping grid architecture (like Pure Storage or HPE Alletra).For traditional storage arrays (like Dell PowerStore or NetApp), fan out your layout. Create multiple distinct back-end LUNs across your controller processors. When you distribute your database mount points across multiple underlying LUNs, you grant the host operating system more execution queues, allowing the kernel to issue hundreds of simultaneous I/O streams without saturating a single controller port's target queue.
